Given project is a hackathon project.
You use:
* litestar as a server
* dishka as a di framework \ ioc container
* adaptix as a data conversion library
* structlog for logging
* uv as the project manager

You stick to clean architecture principles, SOLID and basic DDD rules. We do not use DDD aggregates and complex logic, but claim the pragmatic patterns.
4 main layers are distincted:
* app/infra - Infrastructure layer
* app/presentation - Presentation layer
* app/application - Application layer
* app/domain - Domain (entities) layer

Make sure to scan the codebase for main components if theyre not present in your current context.

Example flow of an operation:
Raw data goes to presentation route, which is validated by a pydantic DTO. Then the route handler invokes an interactor, received from dishka, passing a
DTO as the argument from application layer. Application layer scenarios are distincted by 1. interactors 2. services
An interactor is a user scenario that is supposed to be invoked by the user. A service is a generic component that can be reused by interactors and isnt
limited by a set contract. Services are made as exceptions.
Application layer uses data from domain layer. Data from domain layer is distincted to entities and value objects as per Domain Driven Design.
Application layer uses read-only data gateways to fetch data and unit of work to write data. Written data is passed as entities, and theyre later mapped by 
unit of work to sqla entries.
Data gateway, unit of work, and other protocol implementations reside at the infrastructure layer.

You use modern python 3.12 syntax.
You are provided with a Makefile, which you can use to, for example, check files for code quality at the end of your actions.

Do not make alienated changes to the codebase. This includes comments, docstrings, etc. Current codebase has components for 90% of your needs.
Do not run tests manually, rather delegate this to the user.
Prompt the user for advice \ agreement if youre going to implement something non-standard. Each change youre gonna make is gonna be reviewed,
so make sure to not waste the users time by writing bad code just to complete the prompt.